{
  "what": "Every adapter-probe request this stage ran, with the prefill-trace configuration it ran under and whether its tokens match a reference: the vLLM-integration stage's committed probe where it has that length, otherwise this stage's own tracing-off run of the same length. Compared over the common prefix, because probes ran at different --decode-steps. Reproduce with doc/optimized_vllm/bench/run_discriminators.sh.",
  "conclusions": [
    "Tracing off is correct at every length measured (37, 100, 128, 1024, 4097, 8192).",
    "With tracing on, LONG EAGER prefills can diverge from their first token even though they are not traced. Largest bucket 96 or 128: 4097 and 8192 both diverge. Largest bucket 1024 (alone, as [128,1024], or as the top of a 20-bucket set): 4097 is correct -- but 8192 still diverges, byte-identically to the bucket-128 failure. So a larger largest bucket fixes SOME long lengths and not others.",
    "NO traced configuration measured is correct at every length. That, and not a bucket count, is why tracing ships off.",
    "Capture alone is sufficient: the 4097-ALONE and bucket-96 runs contain no traced request at all -- the bucket is captured at warmup and never replayed -- and 4097 is still wrong.",
    "It is not an unwarmed shape: 8192 IS in PREFILL_WARMUP_LENGTHS.",
    "The repetitive 1024 output (distinct_tokens 4) is a property of the synthetic arange prompt, not corruption: the tracing-off control produces it byte-identically.",
    "Separately, a WIDE bucket set decays SHORT-prompt output into replacement characters after ~22 generations of ordinary traffic (traced_qualitative/, soak_blocking/), which the narrow sets do not.",
    "The mechanism is UNEXPLAINED. The ttnn allocator-lifetime rule accounts for the wide-set decay but not for a capture-only failure with no replay, and nothing measured explains why bucket 1024 fixes 4097 but not 8192."
  ],
